{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794144967,"nonce":"OspRMdKVk+uCHDXJKv8v+/NDYRI=","title":"Mobile frames are GPU fill-bound: the SDF fragment shader costs about 2.5 ns per fragment on an A14","message":"Measured on an iPhone 12 Pro Max (A14, 1284x2778) running mobile/example, release build, 2026-11-08.\n\nWhere a frame went before the fix. Paint walker about 1 ms, element collection 0.4 ms, text prep 2 ms, encode 1.2 ms, then `device.poll(Wait)` blocked for about 38 ms. A Metal System Trace shows one \"Blinc Render Pass\" of about 34 ms of GPU time per frame; its encode is 0.3 ms. So the limit is GPU fill, not the CPU tree walk that 4e110cb blames for the Android frame time.\n\nWhy the pass is slow. The scene is small: 73 primitives (52 core, 21 shadow), about 2.06 screens of core overdraw and 2.0 screens of shadow quads. Repeating one full-screen core fill: 1x 30 ms, 2x 39 ms, 4x 38 ms, 8x 70 ms. Under load the cost settles near 2.5 ns per fragment, far above what a flat blended fill should cost on this GPU. At light load the GPU clock also drops (the trace shows the performance state alternating Medium and Maximum), so removing work from a light frame saves less than it should. Skipping every draw leaves the pass at 1.2 ms.\n\nSuspects, not confirmed: `fs_main` in sdf_core loads the whole GpuPrimitive and runs the clip, gradient, rotation and glass branches for every fragment; `discard` and blending disable hidden-surface removal on a tile GPU, so each overlapping quad is shaded in full.\n\nLanded:\n- `GpuRenderer::poll` no longer waits for GPU idle on iOS and Android. The CPU encodes the next frame while the GPU draws the last, and the swapchain paces the loop. iPhone 19 fps to 35 fps. Pixel 10 Pro about 24 fps to about 60 fps (SurfaceFlinger layer latency, presents every 16.7 ms).\n- mobile/example drops the card drop shadow (flat grouped cards), which removes about 2 screens of blended overdraw.\n\nTo do:\n- Damage rect (3c7afb9): a static screen with a few spinners should shade a small rect, not 4 screens.\n- A plain rounded-rect pipeline with only fill, radius, border and simple clip, chosen per primitive at batch time.\n- Draw a shadow as the ring outside an opaque caster, not under it.\n- Check the Pixel and a low-end Mali with the same method.\n- The offline shader pipeline (709d29f) makes specialised variants cheap to keep in step; native Metal (310506c) gives GPU counters.\n\nMethod, so it can be repeated. `xcrun xctrace record --template 'Metal System Trace' --device \u003cudid\u003e --time-limit 8s --attach \u003cpid\u003e` (launch with `xcrun devicectl device process launch`, pid from `devicectl device info processes`), then `xctrace export --xpath '/trace-toc/run[@number=\"1\"]/data/table[@schema=\"metal-gpu-intervals\"]'` for per-pass GPU time and `metal-application-encoders-list` for encoder spans. The GPU counter and shader profiler tables come back empty unless a counter set is configured. Bisecting draw categories and repeating one draw through temporary environment switches (`devicectl ... --environment-variables`) gave the scaling above.","files":null}]}